Subject: [#94-1-726] SCI:  Lasers/Fusion

> 
> Feel free to explain to me why this won't work, or even why it shows an
> utter lack of understanding of the principles involved.  Pardon my layman's
> terms -- most of the physics I remember, I got from Discover magazine.
> 
> I read recently about how we can "cool" (lower the energy of) an atom down
> to very near absolute zero (a fractional degree Kelvin, I think) by holding
> it still using opposing lasers -- whenever it tries to move in a direction,
> the appropriate one of the lasers nudges it the other way.  I seem to
> recall they could do it with two lasers, but it seemed to me that they
> would need six (not the main topic, but if you can explain why two would
> work, I'd appreciate that, too).

Ok, here goes: Laser trapping and cooling uses lasers tuned just below
a frequency that a particular atom absorbs.  If the atom is moving 
towards one of the laser, that beam appears blueshifted from the point
of view of the atom, which moves the frquency of the laser up to the
absorbtion frequency.  Now the atom absorbs the laser photon and re-emits
it in arandom direction.  Since all the absorbed photons come from one
side and the emitted ones are evenly directed, the net effect is
a momentum transfer (push) away from the beam direction.  Having two
beams means the atoms get pushed back if they go either left or right.
Eventually the atoms come to a stop, and they no longer absorb the
laser light.  Since lack of atomic motion = very cold by defintion, you
get cold gas.  

You are dealing with absorbtion frequencies that involve moving an electron
from one state to another in an atom, typically one electron volt or
thereabouts in energy.  With small atoms, a few tens of electron volts
is sufficient to strip the electrons off the nucleus, giving you ions.

In order to get fusion, you have to get the nucleii close enough together
for the nuclear forces to act.  This requires energies of tens of thousands
of electron volts (the nucleii have to be approaching very fast to get
close together before the positive charges bring them to a stop and then
push them apart again).  So, long before you get to fusion energies, you
lose all the electrons, and laser trapping no longer works.

Your question is not at all dumb, because tokamak approach being used
in the big fusion experiments does something similar to what you suggest,
but using different techniques.  In a tokamak the magnetic fields are
used to hold the particles in place, and microwaves or particle beams
are used to heat them to the point they start to fuse.

Dani Eder


