From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Date: Sun, 30 Jan 1994 17:43:44 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-1-732] PCR: Pancritical Rationalism

Some comments on various postings on Pancritical Rationalism (PCR):

From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
>This "Pancritical Rationalism" has been getting a lot of press here
>lately.  Could anyone consisely yet precisely summarize this point of
>view, in say 500 words or less?  Or is this yet another "philosophy"
>which can only be understood by reading three volumes of the great
>master, ten volumes of commentary by learned students, and only then
>with a full appreciation of the historical context of other views this
>master was reacting to.  (If the latter, I had enough of that in
>academic philosophy, thank you.)

Some complex ideas can only be well considered within their historical 
context.  To some extent this is true of PCR since it is intended as a 
response to problems in other versions of rationalism.  However, rather 
little writing exists strictly on PCR.  The works of Karl Popper -- such as 
The Logic of Scientific Discovery (which can be referred to as "Popper's 
LSD"); Conjectures and Refutations; and Objective Knowledge --which I 
suspect Robin is familiar with, are the closest precursors to PCR.  The only 
book on the topic by a single author known to me (and the major source) 
is William Warren Bartley III's The Retreat to Commitment.  (Good luck 
finding a copy; it's out of print and the USC library didn't have it.  I had 
to borrow MarkM's copy.)  There's also a collection of essays by Gerard 
Radnitsky & W.W. Bartley, III, eds.  (1987).  Evolutionary Epistemology, 
Theory of Rationality, and the Sociology of Knowledge.  (Open Court.)

Robin Hanson:
> Folks have told me it comes down to "leave everything open to
> question, except of course for things you're absolutely sure of (like
> math proofs)".  But surely there must be more than this tautology.

Leave out the second clause "except..." and that's a reasonable way to 
begin a characterization of PCR.  Being absolutely sure of something does 
_not_ exempt a belief, practice, or procedure from PCR's strictures.  _All_ 
beliefs are to be left open to criticism, although Bartley holds that the law 
of noncontradiction has to be retained if any coherent argument is to take 
place (otherwise no argument could ever constitute a criticism of any 
proposition).  Fuzzy logic proponents would even modify that view, 
though maintain its essentials.

I've been unable to find Steve Witham's fine posting explaining PCR, but 
I think he already clarified the important points of PCR.  I will just stress 
here that crucial to PCR is the separation of criticism from justification.  
Previous rationalist views fused criticism and justification in that criticism 
of a position proceeded by showing (immediately or ultimately) that the 
position could not be justified in terms of the rationalist's favorite 
criterion of justification.  For Descartes, this criterion was "clear and 
distinct ideas" (themselves guaranteed by God, whose existence was 
certain because we can perceive the idea of God clearly and distinctly....), 
for Hume every idea had to be traceable back to sense impressions, and 
for logical positivists they had to be traceable to sense-data.  For Rand's 
Objectivists, ideas must be justified in terms of (necessarily valid) senses 
using logic, and ultimately backed by certain axiomatic concepts (more on 
this below).  PCR was the first to state that no belief needs to be justified 
in this way.  Instead, rationality consists in being open to criticism.

From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
>2) beliefs are never true or false, just more or less credible (this
>is the generalization on Popper).  Thus, "falsifying" observations
>reduce the credibility of a belief (presumably by a lot).  In
>addition, heuristics can reduce the credibility of ideas by a lot
>("Rifkin said it, so it's probably bogus.")  This makes for a much
>richer toolset than mere falsification, better models how we actually
>manage beliefs, and allows rationalis to apply to non-objective things
>like ethics (there are more and less effective sets of ethics, for
>example, even though falsification in that domain is tough).  When
>doing things in the world, act on the basis of the beliefs with the
>most credibility.

I agree with what Dean says in the latter part of the above paragraph, but 
I'm uncomfortable with the statement that "beliefs are never true nor 
false".  I don't remember Bartley putting it that way, and it doesn't seem 
to me to be something that PCR need say.  PCR tells us to hold every 
belief open to criticism and possible revision, but it says nothing about 
whether any beliefs _are_ true or false.  Belief A may, in fact, be true. It's
just that we can never know for certain whether A is true (here is a 
contrast with Objectivism).  PCR can happily coexist with realism (or 
"objectivism" with a small "o") since it's a view about when our beliefs 
are rational, not about whether they are in fact true or false.  PCR says 
"you can never be absolutely certain that a belief is true (since we cannot 
be certain about our means of knowledge)"; it does not say "all beliefs are 
only partly true, if at all".

A caveat in regard to Dean's use of "falsification" in the above quote.  
Bartley explains (plausibly it seems to me) that the falsification of a theory 
is always _relative_ to the conflicting data.  Nothing is _ever_ falsified in 
an absolute sense.  If data D conflicts with theory T, we can either reject T, 
reject D, or keep testing both.  PCR is a general epistemology, saying 
nothing about the specifics of how to test theories, leaving this up to 
other, more specific, theories to work out.

Dean again:
>4) Hold all beliefs tentatively--nothing is true, only very credible.

As above, PCR does not claim that nothing is true; it holds only that we 
cannot know for certain that any particular belief is true.


Tim Starr:
>> 1) ...It seems to fail to address the question of why
>> it should be considered "credible," on its own terms.

Steve responds:
>It says, don't expect justification for *anything*, only hold things open 
>to criticism.  Nothing *should* be considered credible on PCR's terms.
>So the way PCR doesn't offer justification for itself isn't contradictory, 
>it's a good example!

I agree with Steve's comment.  I'd add that rationalists can be motivated 
to adopt PCR in place of Panrationalism or Critical Rationalism by coming 
to understand how PCR, unlike other rationalist views, can avoid making 
special exceptions.  Other forms of rationalism merely replace one 
authority (the Bible, mystical insight, the Pope, the Blood, the Fuehrer) 
with others (sense experience, induction, scientific method).  PCR does 
away with _all_ epistemological authorities, holding _every_ belief -- 
including those about the means of acquiring knowledge -- open to 
genuine challenge and revision.

Bartley makes a stimulating analogy that I'd like to extend.  He makes a 
comparison with the democrats attack on traditional political authorities.  
They attacked the legitimacy of the King or religious leader or other 
autark by appealing to the will of the people.  This had salutary effects 
(just as the appeal to the authority of science helped to disembowel 
religious authorities) but also set up yet another authority.  The new 
democratic system might lead to rotten results.  Thus the PCR-like 
question for political authority is not "With what authority shall we 
replace the current political authority", but rather "How can we arrange 
our political institutions so as to be able to get rid of bad rulers once they 
appear?"  Our (Extropian) answer to this involves the use of polycentric 
legal systems.  Polycentric law might be compared to PCR in this way.


