>philosophy which seems to escape the criticisms PCR makes of what 
>Kelley calls "Cartesian Empiricism."  It does present reasons for me to 
>adopt it.  What reason do I have to prefer PCR?

I don't think the position you hold (Rand as revised by Kelley, right?) 
does escape PCR's criticisms.  I'd prefer to hold off on this area, to 
maintain the freshness of my Extro 1 paper, but I'll just say that the view 
you maintain holds some concepts to be axiomatic, i.e, undeniable, 
unrevisable and irrefutable.  Supposed examples are "identity", 
"existence", and "consciousness".  Objectivist epistemology also relies on 
the validity of the senses, using sense data as a foundation for knowledge.  
PCR is motivated by the failure of every attempt to find foundations for 
knowledge.  This doesn't mean that PCR _rejects_ sense experience as 
_one_ means of acquiring knowledge, just that it doesn't ultimately 
privilege any particular epistemic means.

>PCR doesn't talk much about idea generation, only the importance
>of testing.

>Precisely my point.  PCR seems to only address half the problem.  As 
>Rand pointed out, this is one of the classic mistakes of empiricism:  
>"How did the concepts get here?  Somehow."

How is this a "mistake'?  PCR doesn't purport to be a theory of 
everything relevant to knowledge.  PCR says nothing, for instance, about 
neuroscience, even though this is likely to be relevant to understanding 
how we learn and evolve our understanding of the world.  PCR leaves 
room for other co-existing theories to tackle these questions.  Bartley 
recommends thinking of PCR as a non-justificationist _metacontext_ for 
rationality (the two other metacontexts being justificationism and non-
attachment).  A more particular theory about how we come to acquire 
beliefs, generate ideas, and so on, is a context that can exist within the 
metacontext of non-justificationism (PCR).


From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 94 15:18:57 PST
Subject: [#94-1-656] [#94-1-498] Pancritical Rationalism

Dean says:
>>things don't need to be justified (in terms of axioms) to be believed.  

>Now how do I translate "justified"?  Sounds like he means "implied by
>axioms".  But since folks believe contradictory things, which cannot be
>axiomitized, then of course they can believe things which can't be
>axiomitized.  And any non-contradictory set of beliefs can be
>axiomitized (axioms found which imply them all), so this doesn't
>distinguish much.  I guess I'll translate Dean as saying that he
>doesn't see much value in axiomitizing beliefs.

I think that Robin and Dean are using "axioms" in different senses, both 
perfectly good.  Dean means axioms in the sense of propositions (or 
epistemic procedures) that are not open to challenge.  For instance, Rand's 
axiomatic concepts, or a religious authority.  Robin uses axiom to mean 
any proposition that is used to imply other propositions.  The kind of 
axioms that I take Robin to be referring to can still be held open to 
challenge, and so are not axioms in the offensive (to PCR) sense.  Robin's 
axioms are only axiomatic within a certain domain, but can still be 
criticized from a more inclusive perspective.

