Doug spent the past two days attending the hearings that the Patent and
Trademark Office has been holding in San Jose on the issue of software
patents.  I thought you night find his reports interesting.  Feel free to share
with anyone you think might find this relevent.

From: muchomedia@aol.com
To: chip@netcom.com
Date: Wed, 26 Jan 94 23:00:48 EST
Subject: Trip Report: PTO

Today in San Jose, Assistant Secretary of Commerce and Commissioner of
Patents and Trademarks Bruce Lehman chaired the first of two days of public
hearings on patent protection for software-related inventions. In his opening
remarks, he said "These concerns range from claims that the patent system is
incompatible with software development to skepticism over the ability of the
Patent and Trademark Office to accurately gauge innovation in this field of
technology." He mentioned PTO's role in directing some of the electronic
superhighway development. He also said "We will be changing the patent term
to run 20 years from the date of filing, rather than 17 years from the date
of grant."

At issue of course is the fact that the PTO has been granting patents for
methods in the public domain. The PTO acknowledges that this is a problem,
and is taking steps to improve the patent examination process. These hearings
are a part of that reform.

Attendance was much lighter than I expected. There were overwhelmingly more
power ties and tasseled loafers than t-shirts and jeans.

The witnesses could be divided into 5 classes:

1) Attorneys who were grandstanding. Typically, they couldn't understand why
the software industry was having a problem with the patent system. It works
for them.

2) Top of their growth curve, not strictly software companies (Exxon, Apple,
AT&T, Intel), who are looking for a haven of protectionism. Basically, you
don't throw the baby out with the bathwater, even if the baby is dead.

3) Software companies, who are the victims (or expect to soon be victims) of
lousy patents (Borland, VideoDiscovery, Adobe, Mammoth, Oracle). Given a
choice between the current patent system and no system, they would eagerly
like there to be no system. They want to be judged in the marketplace, not in
the courts.

4) Nerds. A programmer got up but was completely unprepared. He called for no
patents, but his arguments were bizarre. The representative from the League
for Programming Freedom was so nervous he sounded like a goat. Or maybe he
always talks like a goat. It's hard to tell with some of these computer
types.

5) Paul Heckel. He is one of these guys who was given a patent for a
metaphor. He has been busy trying to establish its nuisance value, shaking
down Apple, IBM, Oracle, etc. He formed the Abraham Lincoln Patent Holders
Association, which compares the League for Programming Freedom to slaveholders:

 ...in trying to abolish slavery, Lincoln found himself
 countering arguments that outlawing slavery abridged
 one's /freedom/ to own slaves. The League for 
 Programming /Freedom/ by its very name similarly tries
 to justify appropriating the work of others by
 claiming a freedom.

The hearing continues tomorrow at 9:00 at the San Jose Convention Center. It's
free! See you there.


From: muchomedia@aol.com
To: chip@netcom.com
Date: Thu, 27 Jan 94 23:58:57 EST
Subject: PTO, part ii

Today was the close of the hearings in San Jose. They followed the same
pattern as yesterday.

All of the lawyers who testified spoke in favor of the patent system. Not one
admitted to asking himself if the current system was in the best interest of
his clients. It is clearly in the best interest of the attorneys, a fact
which escaped no one.

Folks on the software side were more open. Most, but not all of the
programmers who testified were against software patents. Most applied their
problem solving skill to the issue.

The companies were pretty evenly split between defensive companies that want
protection (IBM) and offensive companies that don't (Sun). Commissioner
Lehman was surprised to hear Microsoft's representative line up on the
defensive side. He was acting under the mistaken understanding that Microsoft
was a company that values innovation. If he had been a regular member of the
Muchomedia Conference, he would have been better informed.

Commissioner Lehman described the current system as a protection racket, and
himself as an incorruptible prosecutor who was going to fix things. He said
that as Commissioner of Patents and Trademarks, he has the power to call any
patent into review. He said that he might do so if he receives enough mail
indicating a problem. This would become a new channel for challenging a lousy
patent which would use the Internet to identify problems at significantly
lower cost than existing remedies.

While he stopped short of promising to ask Congress to abolish the PTO, he
did promise that there 
would be real and substantive changes. We can talk to this guy.

