From: davisd@auburn.ee.washington.edu
Date: Mon, 31 Jan 94 11:55:31 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-737] SEMANTICS: Faith 

> From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
>
> ... Now imagine you were looking at an area [ of neurons ]
> being trained to recognize crows. The neurons in this area might
> bounce around wildly, but each time the person sees a black crow, the
> the value will be pushed towards a stable equilibrium forcing the person
> to believe with high confidence that the next crow he will see is black.

This kind of analysis leads one into conceptual errors pretty quickly.
Neurons are not "being trained to recognize crows". They are part of a
self organizing system without predetermined structural relationship
to the rest of the world.

Further, what meaning can we attach to "believe with high confidence
the next crow he will see is black"? By whose category of crow are we
going to make this judgment?  Lets take it from the viewpoint of our
crow watcher. He has abstracted from specific birds he has seen to a
concept, and has labelled that concept crow. He now sees a white bird
that has a lot of the characteristics that his crow abstraction has.
If his crow abstraction included color as a characteristic, he will
probably be surprised at this white bird, and may revise his crow
abstraction. If not, he will just see another crow.

In short, our bird watcher is not attempting to match his concept of
crow to a crow Ideal, he is grouping his experience. Depending on how
he has grouped his experience, a bird with a lot of the
characteristics of what we call crows, except that it is white, may or
may not surprise him.

